                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"}> algebra
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"}> associative unital algebra
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
                  %h2#ring_theory Ring theory
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"}> ring
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"}> local ring
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
          %h1#fields Fields
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#constructive"} Constructive notions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#category"} Category of fields
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#AccSketch"} Accessibility and sketchability
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A field is a mathematical structure with the usual arithmetic operations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/addition"} addition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtraction"} subtraction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication"} multiplication
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division"} division
            of non-zero elements.
          %p
            The original concept of a field was developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gustav+Lejeune+Dirichlet"} Gustav Lejeune Dirichlet
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            in 1871 under the name “body” (Körper in German), and referred solely to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subfields"} subfields
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DirichletDedekind71"} Dirichlet &amp; Dedekind 1871
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heinrich+Weber"} Heinrich Weber
            generalized the notion of field to today’s definition of field as an arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+ring"} division ring
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Weber93"} Weber 1893
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E.+Hastings+Moore"} E. Hastings Moore
            in 1893 (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Moore93"} Moore 1893
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p Classically:
          #classical.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong field
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              in which every nonzero element has a multiplicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \neq 1
              (which may be combined as: an element is invertible if and only if it is nonzero).
          %p
            Fields are studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %em field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
          %p
            If we omit the commutativity axiom, then the result is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skewfield"} skewfield
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+ring"} division ring
            (also in some contexts simply called a “field”). For example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+%28algebra%29"} free field
            of Cohn and Amitsur is in fact noncommutative.
          %h3#constructive Constructive notions
          %p
            Fields are (arguably) not a purely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            notion in that they don't form an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+category"} algebraic category
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#category"} discussion below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            (including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+field"} discrete field
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+field"} Heyting field
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Heyting+field"} weak Heyting field
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock+field"} Kock field
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+local+ring"} weak local ring
          %p
          #discrete.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              If we replace “an element is invertible iff it is nonzero” in Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#classical"}
              by “an element is invertible
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/xor"} xor
              it equals zero” (which is equivalent in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
              but stronger in
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive logic
              = succeed "." do
                %strong discrete field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              or invertible, and it also implies that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\neq 1
          %p
            Such a field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is ‘discrete’ in that it decomposes as a coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊔
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \{0\} \sqcup F^\times
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\times
            is the subset of invertible elements). An advantage is that this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"} coherent theory
            and hence also a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone77"} Johnstone
            calls such fields
            = succeed "." do
              %strong geometric fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            (however these are defined), although it is satisfied by the ring of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
            (or even
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+number"} algebraic
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"} finite field
          %p
          #heyting.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              If we interpret ‘nonzero’ in Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#classical"}
              as a reference to a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relation"} tight apartness relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo #
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo #
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x # y
              iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x - y
              is invertible, then we obtain the notion of
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+field"} Heyting field
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring#internal"} here
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo #
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\# 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is invertible.
          %p
            This is how ‘practising’ constructive analysts of the Bishop school usually define the simple word ‘field’. An advantage is that the (located Dedekind)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            form a Heyting field, although (for example) the (less located)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+number"} MacNeille real number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalized
            in as many categories.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              If we replace “an element is invertible iff it is nonzero” in Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#classical"}
              by “an element is noninvertible iff it is zero” (which is equivalent in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
              but incomparable in
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive logic
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Heyting+field"} weak Heyting field
              (cf.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Richman20"} Richman (2020)
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johnstone+residue+field"} Johnstone residue field
              (cf.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Johnstone77"} Johnstone (1977)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residue+fields"} residue fields
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\neq 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y\neq 1
              for all
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            An advantage is that even more versions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            (including the
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+number"} MacNeille real number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
            (in particular, the MacNeille reals have no apartness). Another example of a weak Heyting field include the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+series"} Laurent series
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
          %p
            Every discrete field is also a Heyting field, and every Heyting field is also a weak Heyting field. However, a Heyting field is a discrete field if and only if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+relation"} decidable relation
          %p
            A weak Heyting field is a Heyting field if and only if it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residue+field"} residue field
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone77"} Johnstone (1977)
            comes from the fact that these fields are precisely the residue rings of
            %em weak local rings
            (rings in which the noninvertible elements form an ideal).
          %p
            Counterexamples were remarked above, but to be explicit: The (located Dedekind)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            form a Heyting field which need not be discrete. The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+number"} MacNeille real number
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p
            The three definitions above do not exhaust the possible constructive notions of field. For instance, in
            %a{:href => "#MRR87"} MRR87
            the unadorned word
            %strong field
            is defined like a Heyting field above, but with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo #
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#
            being an arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inequality+relation"} inequality relation
            rather than a tight apartness. If the inequality is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denial+inequality"} denial inequality
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone77"} Johnstone77
            is called a
            %strong field of fractions
            since they are precisely the fields of fractions of “weak
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domains"} integral domains
            %a{:href => "#MRR87"} MRR87
            a
            %strong denial field
            is defined to be a Heyting field with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denial+inequality"} denial inequality
            in which additionally
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0)
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal"} prime ideal
          %p
          #TrivialRingAsAField.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              In
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#LombardiQuitté2010"} LombardiQuitté2010
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \neq 0
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo #
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \# 0
              respectively. With such a definition, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+ring"} trivial ring
              counts as a discrete field as well as a Heyting field and constitutes the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
              of such fields.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#category Category of fields
          %p
            Fields are not as well-behaved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} categorically
            as most other common algebraic structures (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"}> groups
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Field"} Field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of fields and field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of the category
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rings"} Rings
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphisms"} ring homomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible
          %p
            In particular, it lacks a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            and also lacks an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            (though it has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+initial+set"} weakly initial set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+fields"} prime fields
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a{:href => "multi-adjoint#MultiInitialObjectsInFields"} multi-initial object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+category"} algebraic
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable
          %h3#AccSketch Accessibility and sketchability
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Field"} Field
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible
            %em finitely
            accessible, and therefore can be presented as the category of models (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketch"} sketch
          %p
            We suppose as given to start with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+sketch"} limit sketch
            whose models are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            denoting the ring. We can construct via limit constructions a subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\hookrightarrow F
            consisting of the invertible elements, as the equalizer of the two maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \times F \;\rightrightarrows\; F,
          %p
            the first being given by multiplication and the second by the composite
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mn 1
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\times F \to * \overset{1}{\to} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            is terminal and the map labeled “1” picks out the element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\in F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↪
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{P &amp; \overset{}{\to} &amp; * \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^0\\
                  I&amp; \hookrightarrow &amp; F,}
          %p
            where the map labeled “0” picks out the element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\in F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is initial (i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is not invertible, or equivalently not equal to
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            or invertible). Of course, in making these last two assertions we use the fact that we are allowing ourselves a limit-colimit sketch instead of just a limit sketch.
          %p
            Note that this gives us the notion of
            %em discrete
            field (see the
            %a{:href => "#constructive"} constructive definitions
            above). The other constructive notions of field can also be described as models for different limit-colimit sketches.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p There are the fields of:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"}> rational numbers
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  (all)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+numbers"} algebraic numbers
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/period"} period
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"}> real numbers
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"}> complex numbers
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+numbers"} p-adic numbers
                  (for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  a
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+number"} prime number
            %p Other examples
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"}> finite field
                  s
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi p
                              %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{F}_{p^n}
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  is a prime
              %li
                %p
                  algebraic
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+field"} number field
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field"}> function field
                  s
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} local ring object
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"} gros Zariski topos
              of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              (given by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \mapsto \Gamma(S, \mathcal{O}_S)
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^{1}_{S}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#classical"}
              holds in the internal logic of this topos. For a proof, see Proposition 2.2 in the article
              %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404976900025"} Universal projective geometry via topos theory
              of Anders Kock. The ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+sheaf"} structure sheaf
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} sheaf topos
              (i.e. the petit Zariski topos) is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residue+field"} residue field
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+scheme"} reduced
              scheme.
